              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %em judgmental equality
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em definitional equality
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Judgmental equality
                is a notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+in+type+theory"} equality in type theory
                which is defined to be a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"}> propositional equality
                ) or a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"}> typal equality
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %strong Definitional equality
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
                on syntactic expressions (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"}> types
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+equality"} syntactic equality
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alpha-equivalence"} alpha-equivalence
          %p
            According to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#PML"} PML (1980), p. 31
          %blockquote
            %p
              Definitional equality is intensional equality, or equality of meaning (synonymy). [] It is a relation between linguistic expressions [] Definitional equality is the equivalence relation generated by abbreviatory definitions, changes of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+variables"} bound variables
              and the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+substituting+equals+for+equals"} principle of substituting equals for equals
          %p on p. 60:
          %blockquote
            %p … intensional (sameness of meaning) …
          %p
            In a similar manner to the usual implicit notion of meta-theoretic conversion and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+conversion"} explicit conversion
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+parametricity"} explicit parametricity
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+dependent+type+theory"} parametric dependent type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                explicit definitional equality, which have a separate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
                to explicitly represent definitional equality in the syntax of the theory itself. The vast majority of dependent type theories have explicit definitional equality.
            %li
              %p
                meta-theoretic definitional equality, which do not have a separate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
                for definitional equality. Examples of such dependent type theories include some flavors of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+type+theory"} objective type theory
          %p
            Explicit definitional equality is also used in single-level type theories like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theory"} higher observational type theory
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conversion+rules"} conversion rules
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+types"} inductive types
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
            to define probe shapes for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"} (infinity,1)-categorical
            types which could not be coherently defined in vanilla dependent type theory.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              There are many different ways that one can conceivably represent definitonal equality explicitly in type theory. The most common approach in the literature defines the definitional equality as an equality judgment, hence the name
              %strong judgmental equality
              for definitional equality. Other possible approaches to definitional equality include adding a second
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
              to the theory and defining the definitional equality explicitly as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
              in the style of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+over+type+theory"} logic over type theory
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Equality judgments can hypothetically be used in type theory for things other than definitional equality, such as a shorthand for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
              in a dependent type theory without only meta-theoretic definitional equality, given by a reflection rule into the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            #ShorthandTypalEquality.maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi a
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi a
                        %mo ′
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ⊢
                        %msub
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi a
                            %mo ′
                        %mo :
                        %mi a
                        %msub
                          %mo =
                          %mi A
                        %mi a
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv a':A}{\Gamma \vdash \delta_{a, a'}:a =_A a'}
            %p
              This article is solely about judgmental equality in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              in the sense of explicit definitional equality defined in the syntax by an equality judgment.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              One may consider adding material to this article about the use of an equality judgment in untyped theories, such as in Reuben Goodstein’s 1954 formalization of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primitive+recursive+arithmetic"} primitive recursive arithmetic
              without
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              for definitional equality.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Judgmental equality of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %li
              %p
                Judgmental equality of types, in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
                with a separate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            %li
              %p
                Judgmental equality of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
          %p
            Judgmental equality of terms is additional structure on types which gives every type the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            in addition to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-groupoidal
            structure on a type from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
          %p
            Judgmental equality of types could be thought of as making explicit the implicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coercion"} coercion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalent types
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtypes"} subtypes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruences"} congruences
          %p
            Judgmental equality of types is not necessary for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with a separate type judgment. It behaves similarly to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijections"} bijections
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-to-one+correspondences"} one-to-one correspondences
            between sets. Similarly, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+isomorphisms"} definitional isomorphisms
            instead of judgmental equality of types.
          %h2#judgmental_equality_of_terms Judgmental equality of terms
          %p Judgmental equality of terms is given by the following judgment:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash a \equiv a' : A
              \-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
              are judgmentally equal well-typed terms of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in context
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            Judgmental equality of terms can be contrasted with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            of terms, where equality is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
            of terms, where equality is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
          %h3#inference_rules Inference rules
          %p Judgmental equality is an equivalence relation:
          %ul
            %li Reflexivity of judgmental equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a:A}{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv a:A}
          %ul
            %li
              %p Symmetry of judgmental equality
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a \equiv b:A}{\Gamma \vdash b \equiv a:A}
            %li
              %p Transitivity of judgmental equality
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a \equiv b:A \quad b \equiv c:A }{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv c:A}
          %p
            In addition, judgmental equality of terms has congruence rules for substitution, the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+substitution"} principle of substitution
          %ul
            %li
              Principle of substitution for judgmentally equal terms:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo :
                          %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo :
                          %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash a \equiv b : A \quad \Gamma, x:A, \Delta \vdash c(x):B}{\Gamma, \Delta(a) \vdash c(a) \equiv c(b): B}
          %p
            If there is a separate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
          %p If one has judgmental equality of types, then the principle of substitution into type families is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash a \equiv b : A \quad \Gamma, x:A, \Delta \vdash B(x) \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma, \Delta(a) \vdash B(a) \equiv B(b) \; \mathrm{type}}
          %p
            This implies the reflection rule of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality#ShorthandTypalEquality"} equality judgment as shorthand for typal equality
            because one could derive the following rule:
          .maruku-equation
